title: Genesis 2 – Marriage
description: A verse-by-verse walk through Genesis 2: God’s design for marriage from the beginning, with cross references from Jesus, Paul and the prophets.
output: lessons/genesis/genesis-2-marriage.html

=== Introduction
--- Genesis Chapter 2 [title]
overline: Young Adult Sunday School · Genesis Series
sub: Marriage · God’s Design from the Beginning
big: Genesis 2:24 | mark: Therefore shall a man leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave unto his wife: and they shall be one flesh.
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

=== The Sabbath
--- God Rested & Sanctified the Seventh Day
kicker: Gen 2:1–3 · The Sabbath, Creation Complete
scripture: Genesis 2:1-3 | mark: God ended his work which he had made; and he rested on the seventh day from all his work which he had made.; And God blessed the seventh day, and sanctified it:

=== The Formation of Man
--- Man Formed from the Dust
kicker: Gen 2:7–8 · The Formation of Man
scripture: Genesis 2:7-8 | mark: And the LORD God formed man of the dust of the ground, and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life;

--- Man Formed by God, Made in His Image
kicker: Cross references · Genesis 2:7
xr: Genesis 1:26-27 | **The Image of God.** Man is unique among all creation, made in God’s own image. The distinction between male and female is built into that image from the very beginning.
xr: 1 Corinthians 11:7-9 | **Man the Image, Woman the Glory.** Paul grounds male/female roles directly in the Genesis 2 creation order, not culture, but design.
xr: Psalm 139:14 | **Fearfully and Wonderfully Made.** Every person is the direct handiwork of God, personally formed, not accidentally produced.
xr: Job 33:4 | **The Breath of the Almighty.** The breath of God in Genesis 2:7 is not merely biological, it is spiritual. Man alone among creation received this direct animating breath.
callout: Man is not the product of time and chance. He was personally formed and animated by the living God. This dignified origin is the very reason marriage, between two image-bearers, carries such weight.

=== Man’s Purpose
--- Dress It and Keep It
kicker: Gen 2:15–17 · Man’s Purpose & Command
scripture: Genesis 2:15-17 | mark: to dress it and to keep it.; But of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, thou shalt not eat of it: for in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die.

=== Not Good to Be Alone
--- It Is Not Good That the Man Should Be Alone
kicker: Gen 2:18 · The First “Not Good” · Foundation of Marriage
scripture: Genesis 2:18 | mark: It is not good that the man should be alone;; I will make him an help meet for him.

--- “Not Good to Be Alone”: Marriage Throughout Scripture
kicker: Cross references · Genesis 2:18
xr: Proverbs 18:22 | **Finding a Wife.** Marriage is not merely permitted, it is a gift and a sign of God’s favour.
xr: Proverbs 31:10 | **A Virtuous Woman.** The “help meet” God promised is portrayed in Proverbs 31 as a woman of remarkable character and godliness.
xr: Ecclesiastes 4:9-12 | **Two Are Better Than One.** Solomon’s wisdom reflects Genesis 2:18. The aloneness God called “not good” is answered by the companionship God designed in marriage.
xr: 1 Corinthians 7:2 | **Marriage as God’s Provision.** Paul acknowledges both the gift of singleness and that marriage is God’s ordained provision for companionship and purity.
callout: God Himself declared aloneness “not good,” the only time before sin that God pronounced something in creation as lacking. The help meet He provided was His answer: not a servant, but a complement perfectly fitted for the man He had made.

=== Adam’s Authority
--- Adam Names the Animals
kicker: Gen 2:19–20 · Adam’s Authority
scripture: Genesis 2:19-20 | mark: and whatsoever Adam called every living creature, that was the name thereof.; but for Adam there was not found an help meet for him.

=== The Woman
--- God Made He a Woman
kicker: Gen 2:21–22 · Creation of Woman
scripture: Genesis 2:21-22 | mark: and he took one of his ribs, and closed up the flesh instead thereof;; And the rib, which the LORD God had taken from man, made he a woman,

--- Woman Created, the Help Meet
kicker: Cross references · Genesis 2:21–22
xr: 1 Corinthians 11:8-9 | **Woman from Man, for Man.** Paul does not invent a gender hierarchy. He traces it directly to Genesis 2’s creation order.
xr: 1 Timothy 2:13 | **Order of Creation Matters.** The order of creation is cited by Paul as the basis for roles within the covenant community.
xr: Ephesians 5:25-27 | **Husbands Love as Christ Loves.** The husband’s calling is not dominance but sacrificial love, just as Christ gave Himself for the Church.
xr: Proverbs 31:11-12 | **The Help Meet Fulfilled.** The “help meet” of Genesis 2 is portrayed throughout Scripture not as lesser, but as indispensable.
callout: Woman was not an afterthought. She was the crown of a creation that God Himself declared incomplete without her. Taken from man’s side, not his feet nor his head, she stands beside him as companion, counterpart, and co-bearer of the image of God.

=== Bone of My Bones
--- Bone of My Bones, Flesh of My Flesh
kicker: Gen 2:23 · Adam’s Proclamation, First Words About Marriage
scripture: Genesis 2:23 | mark: This is now bone of my bones, and flesh of my flesh:; she shall be called Woman, because she was taken out of Man.

--- “Bone of My Bones”: Union & Recognition
kicker: Cross references · Genesis 2:23
xr: Ephesians 5:28-30 | **Husbands & Their Own Bodies.** Paul directly echoes Adam’s words and applies the same intimacy to Christ’s relationship with the Church. Marriage is a living picture of the gospel.
xr: Song of Solomon 2:16 | **Mutual Belonging.** The Song of Solomon celebrates the joyful recognition of belonging that Adam first expressed.
xr: Genesis 29:14 | **“My Bone and My Flesh.”** The phrase “bone and flesh” becomes a covenant expression of belonging throughout the Old Testament.
callout: Adam’s first words were poetry, a joyful cry of recognition. This was no cold arrangement but a moment of wonder. The first human speech recorded in Scripture is a husband celebrating his wife.

=== Leave, Cleave, One Flesh
--- Leave · Cleave · One Flesh — God’s Design
kicker: Gen 2:24–25 · The Institution of Marriage
scripture: Genesis 2:24-25 | mark: Therefore shall a man leave his father and his mother,; and shall cleave unto his wife:; and they shall be one flesh.

--- Marriage Across Scripture
kicker: Cross references · Genesis 2:24
heading: Leave · Cleave · One Flesh
xr: Matthew 19:4-6 | **Jesus Quotes Genesis 2.** Jesus settles the marriage question by going back to creation: permanent, exclusive, one man and one woman.
xr: Mark 10:6-9 | **From the Beginning of Creation.** Marriage predates the Fall, the Law, and every culture. It is anchored in creation order itself.
xr: Ephesians 5:31-32 | **The Great Mystery.** Paul reveals the ultimate meaning: marriage has always been a picture of Christ’s union with the Church.

--- Marriage Across Scripture, continued
kicker: Cross references · Genesis 2:24
heading: Leave · Cleave · One Flesh
xr: 1 Corinthians 6:16 | **One Flesh Is Not Casual.** “One flesh” is a real and binding union. Sexual union always creates a covenant bond.
xr: Malachi 2:14-15 | **The Lord Witness Between Thee and the Wife.** God Himself is witness to the marriage covenant.
xr: Hebrews 13:4 | **Marriage Honourable.** The New Testament upholds the honor of marriage as God designed it: pure, exclusive, and guarded by God’s own judgment.
callout: Genesis 2:24 is the most-quoted verse in the New Testament on marriage, quoted by Jesus and by Paul. One man leaves his family of origin, cleaves exclusively to one wife, and the two become one new covenant unit. This is what God joined. This is what man must not divide.

=== Coming Next
--- But It Wasn’t to Last…
kicker: Gen 3 · The Fall, Marriage Under Curse
p: God had called it *very good.* Man had a purpose, a garden, a companion, and communion with God. Then came the serpent. Genesis 3 will show what sin does to the marriage God designed, and why redemption must restore what the Fall corrupted.
callout: The story of marriage is not finished at Genesis 2. It runs through every page of Scripture, climaxing in Christ and the Church.
